# THIS REPO IS PUBLIC (github.com/genaforvena/self-adint, visibility PUBLIC — check with
# `gh repo view --json visibility` before trusting this line). Everything tracked here —
# README, PLAN, CLAUDE.md, docs/, tools/, receiver/, ref/, android/ — is world-readable.
# What the patterns below keep out is the RAW CAPTURE, which is where his device identifiers
# and his profile live. They are not a privacy boundary around the whole tree; there is no
# such boundary. Before committing anything derived from a capture, assume a reader on the
# internet: quote an advertising ID at the repo's eight-char convention, never in full, and
# keep IMEI / MSISDN / precise location out of prose entirely.
*.jsonl
*.pcap
.env
data/

# …EXCEPT the published tree. `data/` holds observations of HIS DEVICE and never leaves;
# `public-data/` holds observations of the PUBLIC WEB — which domain answered what, which ad
# wrapper its page carries, from which of our own vantages — made by a throwaway browser
# profile with no history. Nothing in it is about a person.
#
# The two are not separated by care at commit time. They are separated by `tools/adint-publish`,
# which copies rows through an explicit field ALLOWLIST: anything a future schema adds is
# dropped unless it is argued onto the list. A denylist would be a promise about columns that
# do not exist yet, and those are precisely the ones that leak. Never hand-copy a file into
# public-data/ — run the tool, so the boundary stays mechanical and auditable.
!public-data/
!public-data/**

# Android build output — the APK is a build product, not a source artifact
android/.gradle/
android/build/
android/app/build/
android/local.properties

# Byte-cache from importing a sibling tool as a module (tools/adint-frame-stageb does this
# so the detector under test is the one that runs, rather than a copy that rots away from it).
__pycache__/
*.pyc
